+# QA report: "You're subscribed" shown but no email arrives
+
+**Date:** 2026-11-11 · **Build:** newsletter 1.2.0 (commit 3216ca3) · **Browser:** Chromium via Playwright
+
+## Summary
+
+The signup page says **"You're subscribed. Check your inbox for the first issue."** even when the server **rejects** the address. The user is never added to the list, so no email ever arrives. The address is not stored and the user sees no error, so they have no reason to try again.
+
+## Root cause
+
+`public/app.js` awaits `fetch('/api/subscribe', …)` but never checks the response. `fetch` only rejects on network failure, so a `422` response falls through to the success message and `form.reset()`. The `{ error }` body the server sends back is never read or shown.
+
+```js
+await fetch('/api/subscribe', { ... });          // response ignored
+status.textContent = "You're subscribed. ...";   // runs for 201 and 422 alike
+```
+
+## Who is affected
+
+The server (`server.js`, `validate()`) rejects addresses with `422` in two cases that the browser's `<input type="email">` allows:
+
+| Input | Browser validation | Server response | Page shows |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `reader@example.com` | passes | `201 {"ok":true}` | "You're subscribed…" ✅ |
+| `reader+news@example.com` | passes | `422 {"error":"Addresses with + are not supported."}` | "You're subscribed…" ❌ |
+| `reader@example.c0m` (TLD not letters-only, e.g. typos or unusual TLDs) | passes | `422 {"error":"Enter a valid email address."}` | "You're subscribed…" ❌ |
+
+The most likely real-world trigger is **plus-addressing** (`name+tag@gmail.com`), which many people use for newsletter signups.
+
+## Reproduction
+
+1. `node server.js`, then open http://localhost:3000
+2. Enter `reader+news@example.com` and click **Subscribe**
+3. In DevTools > Network, `/api/subscribe` returns **422** with `{"error":"Addresses with + are not supported."}`
+4. The page shows "You're subscribed. Check your inbox for the first issue." and clears the field.
+
+## Automated test
+
+`e2e/subscribe.test.js` (node:test + Playwright; starts `server.js` itself on a random port):
+
+```
+node --test e2e/subscribe.test.js
+```
+
+Current result: **1 pass, 2 fail**
+- ✅ control: a normal address is accepted and the page confirms it
+- ❌ plus-addressed email rejected by the server must not show "You're subscribed"
+  `server answered 422 {"error":"Addresses with + are not supported."} but the page told the user: "You're subscribed. Check your inbox for the first issue."`
+- ❌ an address that passes browser validation but fails server validation shows the error
+  `server answered 422 {"error":"Enter a valid email address."} but the page told the user: "You're subscribed. ..."`
+
+The two failing tests should pass once the client checks `response.ok` and shows `body.error`. The plus-address test also requires the page to show the server's error text.
+
+## Suggested fix (not applied)
+
+- In `app.js`, check `res.ok`. If it is false, show the server's `error` message and keep the field filled.
+- Product decision: decide whether to reject `+` addresses at all. Plus-addressing is valid and common, so accepting it removes the main trigger.
+
+## Other observations
+
+- **The server doesn't send any email.** `server.js` only adds the address to an in-memory `Set` (`subscribers`). This build has no welcome or "first issue" email, and every subscriber is lost when the server restarts. If sending happens in another service, that's fine. If not, even valid signups won't receive a confirmation email "in your inbox", and the success message overpromises.
+- Addresses are trimmed in the client and lowercased before storing. A duplicate signup returns `201` again, which is harmless.
